{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "azuresql-db-faq",
  "domain": "azure-sql-database-container",
  "value": [
    "not-in-training-data",
    "corrects-a-wrong-assumption",
    "changes-too-fast-to-memorize"
  ],
  "correction": "Asked what the container supports, an agent answers from general SQL Server knowledge and is confidently wrong. BACKUP and RESTORE are rejected with Msg 40510, USE against a user database fails with Msg 40508, and the current limitation list is a live document rather than something to recall.",
  "posture": [
    "read",
    "inspect"
  ],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "value_declaration": {
    "rationale": "This skill's kernel is a way to sort a capability question, and every bucket boundary is an engine refusal that only this engine produces. The Azure SQL Database container is a gated private preview and the image is not publicly available, so no model has training data on the product, and the arm of a two-arm value run that ran without this skill would fail for the least interesting reason available: it has never heard of the thing. Running that measurement across all seventeen container skills would spend around a thousand model requests to re-establish what the product's release status already establishes. Carlos Robles accepted value for this collection on 2026-09-04 on this written argument instead. THIS IS A DECLARATION AND NOT A MEASUREMENT, and nothing may report it as one. The argument reaches only the container-specific corrections listed in covers, which are the ones no training data contains. It does not reach general Azure SQL Database and SQL Server feature knowledge, which is exactly what the buckets sort, which is ordinary material an ordinary model already handles, and whether this skill improves an answer there is unmeasured and stays unmeasured. The argument ends when the Azure SQL Database container reaches Public Preview: training data begins to contain the product, the premise that nothing could know it stops being true, and this skill has to be measured or demoted. AMENDED 2026-09-19, TEXT ONLY, AND THIS SENTENCE IS THE RECORD OF IT. One line in covers quoted two error numbers that had never been read off the engine. The first live run of this skill's probes, on image tag 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5, Edition 'SQL Azure', build 12.0.2000.8, in a container started with no MSSQL_AAD_* configuration, answered Msg 33233 where it said Msg 15007 and Msg 12824 where it said Msg 12844. Both numbers were corrected in place on 2026-09-19 and nothing else in this block was touched. The argument did not move: the engine refuses exactly what this declaration said it refuses, so what Carlos Robles signed on 2026-09-04 still stands. A signed argument that quotes a false error number would be worse than one that carries a correction on its face, which is why the numbers were fixed and this sentence was added rather than the numbers being left to rot or fixed silently.",
    "covers": [
      "BACKUP and RESTORE being refused with Msg 40510, which is the boundary of the managed-service bucket",
      "USE being refused with Msg 40508",
      "the contained user refused with Msg 33233 and CONTAINMENT PARTIAL refused with Msg 12824",
      "BULK INSERT from a local path refused with Msg 12713, because this engine reads bulk data from Azure Blob Storage only"
    ],
    "does_not_cover": [
      "general Azure SQL Database and SQL Server feature knowledge, which is what the buckets sort rather than what they add",
      "how to phrase a hedged answer when a question does not resolve"
    ],
    "decided_by": "Carlos Robles",
    "decided_on": "2026-09-04",
    "expires_when": "the-container-reaches-public-preview"
  },
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db-container"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "container",
    "assert": [
      "answers from the documented limitation list",
      "links the live known-limitations page rather than reciting from memory"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition, CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS nvarchar(128)) AS edition;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              5,
              "SQL Azure"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The first bucket in the skill's mental model, made executable: this is the Azure SQL Database engine and not the SQL Server image, which is the premise every other answer in the skill rests on. If this returns anything else, the four bucket sort is being applied to the wrong product."
      },
      {
        "sql": "BACKUP DATABASE master TO DISK = N'/var/opt/mssql/data/probe_faq_backup.bak';",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40510
        },
        "why": "The most asked question in the skill, and one it answers with a specific number in three places: SKILL.md, references/faq.md and references/limitations.md all say Msg 40510. The database named is master rather than the scratch database because the statement filter refuses the statement before it resolves the name, and the skill's own claim is that this fails in every session."
      },
      {
        "sql": "RESTORE DATABASE master FROM DISK = N'/var/opt/mssql/data/probe_faq_backup.bak';",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40510
        },
        "why": "The skill claims the same number for RESTORE as for BACKUP, and the two are worth separating because the answer people act on is the restore half: it is what somebody reaches for after losing a container. If RESTORE were merely failing because the file does not exist, this probe would report a file error instead, which is itself the signal that the statement is not being filtered."
      },
      {
        "sql": "USE master;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40508
        },
        "why": "The second most asked question, and the one whose answer is easiest to get half right. The refusal is a property of the session, not of the engine: this probe runs in the scratch user database and must fail, whereas the same statement on a master connection succeeds because master is a provisioning session. A pass here also confirms the runner is not accidentally connected to master, which would quietly invalidate the two probes above."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE USER probe_faq_contained_user WITH PASSWORD = 'Pr0be_Passw0rd!x';",
        "expect": {
          "error": 33233
        },
        "why": "The answer here is inverted from Azure SQL Database, which is what makes it worth a probe: in the cloud a contained user is the recommended shape, and on the container it fails, so the correct guidance is a server login plus a mapped user. THE NUMBER WAS WRONG UNTIL 2026-09-19: this skill's SKILL.md and references/faq.md both said Msg 15007, and the engine says Msg 33233, \"You can only create a user with a password in a contained database.\" Body and probe were corrected together. The password in this probe is a throwaway for a user that never exists. Re-measured 2026-09-19 on image tag 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5, Edition 'SQL Azure', build 12.0.2000.8, in a scratch container started with no MSSQL_AAD_* configuration."
      },
      {
        "sql": "ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CONTAINMENT = PARTIAL;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 12824
        },
        "why": "The obvious workaround for the refusal above, and it is also refused, which is why the skill's answer sends people to a login and a mapped user rather than to a database setting. The engine says Msg 12824, \"The sp_configure value 'contained database authentication' must be set to 1 in order to alter a contained database.\", then Msg 5069, \"ALTER DATABASE statement failed.\" THE NUMBER WAS WRONG UNTIL 2026-09-19: this skill said Msg 12844 with the text \"this functionality is not available in the current edition of SQL Server\", which no run of this probe has ever produced. Without this probe the previous one invites the reader to try the containment setting and conclude the skill was simply missing a step. Re-measured 2026-09-19 on image tag 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5, Edition 'SQL Azure', build 12.0.2000.8, in a scratch container started with no MSSQL_AAD_* configuration."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_faq_bulk (col1 nvarchar(100) NULL);\nBULK INSERT dbo.probe_faq_bulk FROM '/var/opt/mssql/data/probe_faq_nonexistent.csv' WITH (FIELDTERMINATOR = ',', ROWTERMINATOR = '\\n');",
        "expect": {
          "error": 12713
        },
        "why": "A local file is not a valid source: the engine reads from Azure Blob Storage, so the loading answer for a laptop CSV is a client side tool and not BULK INSERT. Msg 12713 is stated in this skill's references/faq.md. The file deliberately does not exist, which is the point: the statement is refused for being local before anything tries to open it, so a file-not-found error here would mean the restriction has been lifted and the skill's answer needs rewriting."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the azuresql-db-faq skill"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "can I take a backup of this container?"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "set up a local PostgreSQL database with docker compose",
      "provision an Azure SQL Database in my subscription",
      "connect to SQL Server on my company network"
    ]
  }
}
